@startuml csr_signing_key_reference
title CSR Signing: Bound Key with Eager / Batch / Standalone DER Input
hide footbox
autonumber
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam ArrowColor #46535E
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam maxMessageSize 260
skinparam sequence {
  LifeLineBorderColor #94A3B8
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  ParticipantBorderColor #596B78
  ParticipantFontColor #20313D
  ActorBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  ActorBorderColor #596B78
  GroupBorderColor #596B78
  NoteBackgroundColor #FFF9DC
  NoteBorderColor #C4A15C
}

actor Application as App
box "Client process" #E8F1FF
  participant "ICryptoContext\nclient factory" as Factory
  participant "ICsrGenerationContext\nclient proxy" as CsrClient
end box
box "Crypto daemon" #F4F7F9
  participant "Mediator\nselection and assembly" as Daemon
  participant "KeyManagementService" as Keys
  participant "Provider / CSR handler\nexisting OpenSSL implementation" as Provider
  participant "CsrProcessingExecutor\nbackend-independent RPC contract" as Executor
  participant "ICsrProcessor\nOpenSSL adapter to tested native helper" as Processor
  participant "wire::Decode\nowned input snapshot" as DerWire
  participant "PopulateRequestFromDer\nstrict Name / Extensions" as DerInput
end box

group 1. Obtain a private-key reference
  App -> Factory : ResolveResource(configured_name, kKeySlot)
  Factory -> Daemon : RESOLVE_RESOURCE
  Daemon -> Keys : resolve configured slot for this client
  Keys --> Daemon : client-scoped key-slot node
  Daemon --> Factory : resolved resource handle
  Factory --> App : CryptoResourceId key_ref
  note over App, Factory
    Alternatively, use an existing kKey reference returned
    by key generation or loading on this connection.
    The application holds a handle, not private-key bytes.
  end note
end

group 2. Bind that key when creating the CSR context
  App -> App : config.SetKey(key_ref)\n.SetAlgorithm(signature_algorithm)
  App -> Factory : CreateCsrGenerationContext(config)
  Factory -> Daemon : CTX_CREATE(CSR_GENERATION, algorithm, ...)\nparameter[3] = key_ref.id
  Daemon -> Keys : ResolveTargetProvider(client, preference, key_ref.id)
  Keys --> Daemon : key-compatible provider
  Daemon -> Provider : create CSR handler for signature algorithm
  Provider --> Daemon : uninitialized handler
  Daemon -> Keys : BindAndAuthorize(client, context_id, key_ref.id,\nprovider, permission = kSign, algorithm)
  note right of Keys
    Check ownership, signing permission,
    algorithm and provider compatibility.
    Load/share a slot key if needed.
    Retain a context-owned key reference.
  end note
  Keys --> Daemon : bound key handler + resolved key node
  Daemon -> Provider : InitializeContext(bound_key_handler, context_id, ...)
  Provider --> Daemon : initialized
  Daemon --> Factory : context_id
  Factory -> CsrClient : construct proxy with context_id and config
  CsrClient --> Factory : created proxy
  Factory --> App : CSR context
end

group 3. Choose input preparation; processing never uses the bound key
alt [E] Eager setters
  App -> CsrClient : SetSubjectDn(...) / optional extension setters
  loop Existing helper / validation requests as needed
  CsrClient -> Daemon : CSR_PROCESS on this same context_id\nexisting readable / writable SHM spans
  Daemon -> Provider : authenticate context ownership and forward
  Provider -> Executor : own context's Execute(spans)
  Executor -> Executor : validate spans; wire::Decode\nreject Generate and GenerateFromDer
  Executor -> Processor : Process(owned Document)
  Processor --> Executor : processed Document / mapped daemon error
  Executor -> Executor : wire::Encode; capacity / response checks\nthen copy output bytes
  Executor --> Provider : SHM output + one uint64 byte count
  Provider --> Daemon : processing response
  Daemon --> CsrClient : byte count
  end
  CsrClient -> CsrClient : commit accepted setter values
  CsrClient --> App : accepted setter values
else [B] ConfigureBatch: one whole-request validation IPC
  App -> CsrClient : ConfigureBatch(batch)
  CsrClient -> CsrClient : collect all field descriptions locally
  CsrClient -> Daemon : CSR_PROCESS on same generation context_id\nSCR1 ValidateBatch + all fields; SHM buffers
  Daemon -> Provider : authenticate ownership and forward
  Provider -> Executor : own context's Execute(spans)
  Executor -> Executor : span / wire checks; reject both generation actions
  Executor -> Processor : Process(owned ValidateBatch Document)
  Processor --> Executor : complete normalized Document / mapped error
  Executor -> Executor : on success: encode / capacity / response checks\nthen output copy
  Executor --> Provider : SCR1 + written count / error
  Provider --> Daemon : processing response
  Daemon --> CsrClient : response
  alt Successful, complete and well-formed response
    CsrClient -> CsrClient : atomically replace snapshot; clear eager SAN cache
    CsrClient --> App : accepted; separate Generate() still required
  else Rejected batch or malformed response
    CsrClient -> CsrClient : preserve previous snapshot
    CsrClient --> App : error; no partial commit
  end
else [D] Standalone DER input
  App -> App : prepare subject_dn_der and optional extensions_der\nskip all processing / setter calls
end
end

opt 4. Application explicitly requests signing using the already-bound key
alt [G] Current accepted eager / batch snapshot
  App -> CsrClient : Generate() (separate from ConfigureBatch)
  CsrClient -> Daemon : CSR_GENERATE_NATIVE on context_id\nCSR input + output buffer; no new key selection
else [D] One-operation DER generation
  App -> CsrClient : GenerateFromDer(subject_dn_der, extensions_der)
  CsrClient -> CsrClient : bounds / serialization only\nno DER parsing or builder mutation
  CsrClient -> Daemon : SAME CSR_GENERATE_NATIVE on context_id\nSCR1 GenerateFromDer=17 + DER fields; SHM buffers
end
  Daemon -> Provider : forward operation to bound context handler
  Provider -> Provider : create operation-local X509_REQ with bound public key
  alt [D] Strict standalone DER ingress
    Provider -[#B45309]> DerWire : D1: Decode(input); verify owned wire snapshot
    DerWire --[#B45309]> Provider : D1r: verified DER-only Document
    Provider -[#B45309]> DerInput : D2: validate both structures, then populate request
    DerInput --[#B45309]> Provider : D2r: populated fields
  else [G] Structured input
    Provider -> Provider : decode / revalidate snapshot\nencode extensions with bound SPKI; populate request
  end
  Provider -> Provider : after successful validation: sign with bound key\nverify signature; encode DER; check capacity\nallocate response, then publish output
  Provider --> Daemon : signed CSR DER in output buffer + byte count
  Daemon --> CsrClient : written-byte count
  CsrClient --> App : ICsrExport containing signed CSR DER
end

note over App, Provider
  The key reference is passed at context creation, not embedded in PKCS#10.
  PKCS#10 carries the public key and signature, never private-key bytes.
  A different key or algorithm requires a new CSR context.
  This signing operation does not use CSR_PROCESS or a generic hardware signer.
  Batch validation is not authorization for later signing; Generate revalidates.
  Failed batches never trigger signing; a later explicit Generate uses the last accepted snapshot.
  Generation shows success: validation errors return immediately without signing or publishing output.
  DER generation inherits no builder state and needs no standalone processing context.
end note
@enduml
